        <div id="what-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Is a Solar Charger? A Folding Panel With USB Ports and No Battery
          </h2>

          <p>
            A solar charger, in the sense this guide uses, is a folding fabric or laminated panel of about 15 to 30 watts with USB ports built into a small junction box. You unfold it in the sun, plug in a phone, tablet or power bank, and it charges directly. There is no battery inside, so it stores nothing: when the sun goes down or a cloud passes, output drops to zero or close to it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Solar Charger vs Solar Power Bank</h3>

          <p>
            A solar power bank is a battery pack with a small panel glued to its face. It stores energy, which is useful, but its panel is tiny. A folding solar charger has many times more panel area, so it actually collects meaningful energy from the sun. Our guide to the <a href="/best-solar-power-bank/">best solar power banks</a> covers the battery side, and recommends exactly the combination this guide is about: a folding panel paired with a good power bank. If the device you need to keep running is a laptop, look at a <a href="/best-laptop-power-bank/">laptop portable charger</a> with enough USB-C PD wattage to match it, and expect a 20 to 35 watt panel to refill it slowly over a full day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Solar Charger vs Portable Solar Panel</h3>

          <p>
            Larger portable panels of 60 to 200 watts are designed to charge portable power stations through DC or XT60 connectors. They are heavier, bigger and often lack USB ports altogether. If you are trying to recharge a power station for camping or a blackout, see our picks for the <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">best portable solar panels</a> instead. This guide sticks to the small USB chargers built for phones, tablets, headlamps, GPS units and power banks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Who Actually Needs One</h3>

          <p>
            Backpackers on trips longer than a power bank lasts, campers at sites without hookups, boaters, and anyone building an emergency kit for multi-day outages. If you only go out for a weekend, a large power bank alone is usually simpler.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-power-bank" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why a Folding Solar Charger Beats a Built-In Panel Power Bank for Real Solar Charging
          </h2>

          <p>
            The difference comes down to surface area. Sunlight delivers roughly 1,000 watts per square meter at its very best, and good consumer cells convert around 20 to 25 percent of that. A panel the size of a phone's back can only collect a watt or two even in perfect summer sun. A 21 to 28 watt folding charger is many times larger, and output scales with area.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Arithmetic</h3>

          <p>
            A 10,000mAh power bank stores about 37 watt-hours. At one or two watts from a built-in panel, filling it would take days of ideal sun. A folding charger that delivers even 8 to 12 real watts in good sun can put the same energy in over a long afternoon, allowing for conversion losses. Those are rough estimates, since real output depends on sun, angle and heat, but the gap is too large for those variables to close.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Built-In Panels Are a Backup Feature</h3>

          <p>
            Solar power bank makers commonly describe the panel as an emergency or backup charging method and recommend charging from a wall outlet first. That is an honest description. A built-in panel will slowly add a little charge to a bank left on a dashboard, but it will not keep a phone running on a week-long trip.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Solar Power Bank Still Makes Sense</h3>

          <p>
            If you want one rugged device with a flashlight for a glovebox or go-bag and you will charge it from the wall, a solar power bank is fine. Just treat the panel as a bonus, not the main power source.
          </p>

