        <div id="watts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Many Watts Do You Need? About 20W for One Phone, 28W or More for Two Devices
          </h2>

          <p>
            Panel wattage is the most important number on a solar charger, but it is a best-case figure measured under ideal conditions. Plan on getting noticeably less in the real world.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rated Watts vs Real Watts</h3>

          <p>
            Ratings assume strong light, a cool panel and perfect alignment with the sun. Heat, haze, low sun angles and a panel lying flat on the ground all cut output. MARBERO's listing is unusually candid, quoting 18 to 21 watts from its 21 watt panel only in full sun at a 35 to 65 degree tilt. Owners commonly report seeing well under half the rated figure on hazy days.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">15 to 21 Watts: One Phone</h3>

          <p>
            A panel in this range, like the FlexSolar 20W or BigBlue SolarPowa 20, will reliably charge one phone in good sun. It is the right size for emergency kits and occasional use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">25 to 30 Watts: Phone Plus Power Bank</h3>

          <p>
            This is the sweet spot for most buyers. The BigBlue 28W and SOLUPUP 30W have enough area to feed a phone and a power bank at the same time, or to fill a power bank faster on its own, and they still fold small enough to carry.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Beyond 30 Watts</h3>

          <p>
            The BigBlue 35W is worth the extra weight only if you will use its USB-C PD port. Above that, panels grow heavy enough that you are better off with a power station panel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="usb-c-pd" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. USB-C PD on a Solar Charger: Why Most Ports Are Limited to 5 Volts
          </h2>

          <p>
            A USB-C port on a solar charger looks like the one on a wall charger, but it usually behaves very differently. Knowing why saves a lot of confusion.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">5V Ports Cap Out Around 15 Watts</h3>

          <p>
            Most solar chargers, including six of our seven picks, have USB ports that output only 5 volts. At the usual 3 amp limit, that is 15 watts per port at most, and phones charge at standard speed rather than fast-charge speed. That is not a defect. A 5V circuit is simpler and copes better with the fluctuating power a panel produces.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What PD and PPS Add</h3>

          <p>
            USB Power Delivery lets a port negotiate higher voltages, such as 9V, for faster charging. PPS, or Programmable Power Supply, fine-tunes the voltage in small steps to reduce heat. The BigBlue 35W lists a 25W PD port with PPS, which suits Samsung and Pixel phones and USB-C power banks that accept PD input.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Apple Caveat</h3>

          <p>
            BigBlue's listing states that its PD port does not support Apple devices. iPhone owners can still charge from the panel's other ports at standard speed, but should not buy the 35W model for fast charging alone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="auto-restart" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Auto-Restart: Why Your Phone Stops Charging When a Cloud Passes
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common complaint about solar chargers is that the phone charges for a minute, stops, and never starts again. It is usually not the panel that failed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Happens When Output Dips</h3>

          <p>
            When a cloud or a shadow covers the panel, voltage drops below what the phone expects. Many phones and power banks treat that as the charger being unplugged. When the sun returns, some panels and devices do not renegotiate the connection, so charging stays stopped until you unplug and reconnect the cable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Panels That Reconnect on Their Own</h3>

          <p>
            Better chargers include a smart chip that restarts output when light returns. SOLUPUP's listing describes exactly this, with steady current and automatic reconnection. BigBlue's 35W model uses a dual parallel circuit so one half of the panel keeps producing if the other half is shaded.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Reliable Workaround</h3>

          <p>
            Charge a power bank from the panel rather than a phone. Power banks are generally more tolerant of fluctuating input, and the phone then charges steadily from the bank. That leads to the setup we recommend.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pairing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Pair Your Solar Charger With a Power Bank: The Setup That Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            A solar charger on its own only works while the sun shines. Add a power bank and you store the day's energy for the evening, smooth out the dips from clouds, and keep the phone out of the hot sun.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choosing the Power Bank</h3>

          <p>
            Look for a bank that accepts 5V USB-C input, because that is what most panels deliver, and around 10,000 to 20,000mAh. A display showing input watts is a real advantage, since it tells you at a glance whether the panel is aimed well. Our guide to the <a href="/best-camping-power-bank/">best camping power banks</a> weighs capacity against weight for trips.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Run the Setup</h3>

          <p>
            Plug the power bank into the panel in the morning and leave it charging all day, shaded under the panel or in the storage pouch. Charge the phone from the bank in the evening or whenever needed. On a sunny day, a 28W panel can typically add a meaningful share of a 10,000mAh bank's capacity.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pass-Through Charging</h3>

          <p>
            Some power banks can charge a phone while they are being charged. It is convenient, but it adds heat and is slower than doing one thing at a time, so on a hot day charge the bank first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How to Get the Most From a Solar Charger: Angle, Heat and Shade
          </h2>

          <p>
            Small habits make a big difference to how much energy a folding panel collects.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Face the Sun Directly</h3>

          <p>
            Output is highest when the panel faces the sun squarely. Prop it up with a kickstand, a rock or a pack, and re-aim it every hour or two as the sun moves. A panel lying flat on the ground in the late afternoon may produce a fraction of its rating.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep the Phone Cool</h3>

          <p>
            Phones stop charging when they get too hot, and a phone sitting in direct sun next to a panel heats quickly. Keep it in the panel's pouch or in the shade of the panel itself, as FlexSolar's and SOLUPUP's listings both advise.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Avoid Partial Shade</h3>

          <p>
            Shading even a small part of a panel can cut output sharply, because cells are wired in series. Keep straps, branches and your own shadow off the cells.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Skip the Window</h3>

          <p>
            Glass reflects and absorbs part of the light. A panel on a dashboard or behind a window will produce noticeably less than the same panel outside.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="emergencies" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Solar Chargers for Power Outages and Emergency Kits
          </h2>

          <p>
            A small solar charger is a cheap, silent addition to an outage kit, but it is worth being clear about what it can and cannot do.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What It Can Do</h3>

          <p>
            Keep phones, a radio with USB charging, headlamps and a power bank going for days when the grid is down. That covers communication and light, which matter most in the first days of an outage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What It Cannot Do</h3>

          <p>
            Run a fridge, a CPAP, a sump pump or anything that plugs into a wall outlet. For those you need a power station with a larger panel or a generator. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-charge-a-portable-power-station-with-solar/">charging a portable power station with solar</a> explains the bigger setup, and our list of the <a href="/best-portable-charger-for-power-outages/">best portable chargers for power outages</a> covers the batteries to pair with a panel.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Storing It in a Kit</h3>

          <p>
            Keep the panel folded with a cable for each device and a charged power bank. Unfold it and test it in the sun once or twice a year so you know it works and that you remember how to aim it. For cloudy days, bank the sun you do catch in a <a href="/best-high-capacity-power-bank/">high capacity power bank</a>, or a magnetic <a href="/best-power-bank-for-iphone/">power bank for iPhone</a> you can top up at midday.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a Built-In Panel to Do the Work</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Buying a solar power bank and leaving it in the sun to recharge leads to disappointment, because its small panel collects only a watt or two. If you need to recharge from the sun day after day, buy a folding solar charger of 20 to 30 watts and use the power bank as storage rather than as the solar collector.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the Wattage on the Label</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 28 watt rating is measured under ideal conditions. In real use, with heat, haze and imperfect angles, output is often half that or less. Size your panel with that in mind, choose 25 to 30 watts if you need to charge two devices, and expect slower charging in the morning, evening and in winter.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging the Phone Straight Into the Panel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Phones often stop charging when a cloud passes and do not restart, and they overheat in direct sun. Charging a power bank from the panel instead gives steadier results, keeps the phone cool and stores energy for the night. If you do charge a phone directly, choose a panel with an auto-reconnect feature.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Laying the Panel Flat and Forgetting It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A panel lying flat on the ground all day misses much of the available sun, especially in the morning and afternoon. Prop it up to face the sun and re-aim it every hour or two. That simple habit can make more difference than buying a bigger panel.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Fast-Charge Panel Without Checking Compatibility</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                USB-C PD on a solar charger only helps if your device supports it, and some panels exclude certain brands. BigBlue's 35W listing, for example, states its PD port does not support Apple devices. Check the port notes before paying for fast charging you cannot use.
              </p>
            </div>
