          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best solar charger?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The BigBlue 28W is the best solar charger for most people. Its listing specifies two USB-C ports and one USB-A port, 5V/4.8A combined output, cells quoted at 25.4 percent efficiency and a 1.5 pound folding design. Android users who want fast charging should look at the BigBlue 35W with USB-C PD, and backpackers at the 0.84 pound BigBlue Ultra-Light.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are solar chargers worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if you spend several days away from outlets, such as backpacking, camping without hookups or riding out a long power outage. A 20 to 30 watt folding charger paired with a power bank can keep a phone running indefinitely in sunny weather. For a weekend trip, a large power bank charged at home is usually simpler and more reliable.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do solar chargers work on cloudy days?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They work, but slowly. Thin cloud may reduce output by half or more, and heavy overcast can drop it to a trickle that some phones will not accept at all. On cloudy days, charge a power bank rather than a phone, since banks generally tolerate low and fluctuating input better, and choose a larger panel if you live somewhere often overcast.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many watts of solar charger do I need for a phone?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About 20 watts is enough to charge one phone in good sun. If you want to charge a phone and a power bank together, or get usable output in less than perfect light, choose 25 to 30 watts. Real output is usually well below the rating, so buying a slightly larger panel than the math suggests is sensible.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my phone stop charging on a solar charger?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually because output dipped when a cloud or shadow passed, and the phone treated it as being unplugged. Some panels do not restart charging on their own. Heat is the other common cause, since phones pause charging when hot. Shade the phone, choose a panel with auto-reconnect, or charge a power bank from the panel instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a solar charger charge a laptop?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most small USB solar chargers cannot charge a laptop usefully, because their ports output only 5 volts and laptops need USB-C Power Delivery at higher voltages and wattages. A panel with a PD port may slowly charge some small USB-C laptops in strong sun. For laptops, it is more practical to charge a power station from a larger panel.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a solar charger charge a power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only very small ones, and slowly. A 21 watt panel with a DC output, like the MARBERO, can trickle-charge a compact power station, but filling even a small unit takes many hours of sun. For power stations, choose a 100 watt or larger portable solar panel with the right connector for your unit's solar input.
              </p>
            </div>

